## Summary
The Mundaneum was an early 20th-century initiative in Mons, Belgium, that aimed to compile the world's knowledge using millions of index cards, acting as a precursor to the modern internet. Visiting this site can alter perceptions of everyday technology by demonstrating historical methods of information organization and retrieval.

## Key Points
- The Mundaneum was established in Mons, Belgium, as a global repository for knowledge in the early 20th century.
- It utilized millions of index cards for data storage and retrieval, representing an innovative form of information technology before the digital age.
- The project offers historical insights into how early knowledge management systems influenced contemporary information retrieval and internet concepts.
- Despite its potential to change views on technology, the Mundaneum may be overlooked today due to its name and perceived dullness.


